.\" Revised Draft prepared from current source; see EDITORS-NOTES.md.
.\" Source: src/monitor.c, src/monitor-data.c, src/monitor-display.c
.TH "MONITOR" "1" "October 23, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
monitor \- listen to and display multicast receiver audio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B monitor
.RI "[options] [data-destination ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
monitor listens on one or more multicast groups for RTP audio streams,
determines their formats from
metadata, decodes and resamples audio for playback, and displays active
sessions with a ncurses interface. By default active sessions are mixed and
positioned across stereo output. Optional voting can instead select only
the strongest channel, eg, among a group of linked repeaters.
A source may be a numeric address or a resolvable domain name.
The
usual RTP data port is UDP 5004; the command/status port is UDP 5006.
A multicast group may contain several streams distinguished by
SSRC.
.P
The following audio encodings are supported: Opus; \[*m]-law PCM; A-law PCM;
16-bit linear big-endian PCM;
16-bit linear little-endian PCM;
IEEE-754 binary16 floating point PCM;
IEEE-754 binary32 big-endian floating point PCM
and IEEE-754 binary32 little-endian floating point PCM.
.SH DISPLAY
The default dashboard uses ASCII frames and terminal colors, with a
highlighted selected channel and a keyboard shortcut bar. Green marks recent
audio activity, yellow marks recent activity below 6 dB SNR, cyan marks
reported digital modes, and dim rows indicate idle or muted sessions.
Color is optional; selection and activity also use text attributes.
.P
The Config column shows the configured demodulator; Detect shows the reported
voice mode, or a dash when none is reported. DMR idle bursts do not select
a voice mode. Frequencies are displayed in MHz. The Tone column displays
detected CTCSS in Hz or DCS as D023/I023 (normal/inverted), including analog
FM calls on mixed channels. Detection does not require notching; digital
voice is excluded to avoid false tone detections. On P25 channels it shows
the NAC last received (N293), a following channel's being its control
channel's.
.P
The Call column shows the call in progress: the talkgroup (TG2000), the
radio a private call is to (U2000) or the destination callsign and the talker: their DMR talker alias once it has
come, a few seconds into the call, else their ID, then any D-STAR text
message or the way a YSF call came (via UPLINK/DOWNLINK), and on a trunked channel the voice
frequency being followed (TG110 463.7250 1757). Between calls a trunked
channel shows its state there: CC R2 or CC N469 while its control channel is
received, no CC when it is lost, CC search while other control channels are
tried, LTR R1 for an LTR home repeater. Last is the time since the channel
last sent audio: now, 4:07 (minutes and seconds), 3h12, 2d.
.P
After a call ends it stays in Call, fading (normal for 30 seconds, then
dim), until another call replaces it or call\-hold seconds have passed
(default 600; call\-hold in the [display] section of the configuration
file); then a trunked channel's state shows again. Call is as wide as the
terminal allows, up to 64 columns. Terminals narrower than 100 columns leave
out Config, SNR and Gain, and a narrow Call the followed frequency.
.P
A D-STAR or YSF call that sends its position (D-STAR's D-PRS or NMEA GPS in
the slow data, the GPS message of a YSF radio such as an FT-3D)
shows the sender's locator after their callsign in Call (CQCQCQ KD2TYY
FN02qu).
.P
Larger terminals include selected-channel details: source, destination,
timeslot, gain, SNR, and packet counters, and the last position heard on the
channel: the sender, the locator and latitude,longitude, the distance and
bearing with \-\-location, and how long since that position came once the
call has ended. radiod sends the positions it decodes on its status stream,
as it does the callsigns and the audio: they are what the stations broadcast,
and D-PRS normally reaches the APRS network too. \-\-location is only
monitor's own point of reference; it is not sent anywhere. Smaller terminals omit this panel
and reduce the table columns. Press v to switch to the full diagnostic table
and h for keyboard help. Existing navigation and audio controls are unchanged.
.P
Above the channels (terminals of 22 lines or more; S hides or shows it) the
receiver summary has a line for each front end: its description and LO
frequency, IF level in dBFS with the highest of the last ten minutes and how
long ago it was, gain (and AGC), A/D overloads since radiod started, in the
last ten minutes, and how long since the last (in yellow while that count
is above zero), and how old these figures are: they come with the
channels' status, which radiod sends while a channel is active. Below it,
the three channels with the most audio in the last ten minutes, and how
much. The ten minutes are counted in steps of ten seconds, so an event can
leave them up to ten seconds early or late.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-I destination, \-\-input destination"
Add an input data group. May be repeated; positional destinations are also
accepted.
.TP
.B "\-o source, \-\-source source"
Restrict reception to one network source.
.TP
.B "\-L, \-\-list\-audio"
List PortAudio devices and exit.
.TP
.B "\-R device, \-\-device device"
Select the PortAudio output device by name or numeric index. Default is the
PortAudio default output.
.TP
.B "\-P pathname, \-\-pipe pathname"
On Linux, write headerless native-endian signed 16-bit PCM to an existing
named pipe instead of the local audio output device.
Rate and channel count follow the
playback settings. Opening the pipe can wait for a reader.
.TP
.B "\-r rate, \-\-samprate rate"
Set playback sample rate in Hz. Default 48000.
.TP
.B "\-c channels, \-\-channels channels"
Select one or two playback channels. Default two.
.TP
.B "\-b frames, \-\-blocksize frames"
Set PortAudio callback block size in sample frames. Default 960.
.TP
.B "\-g dB, \-\-gain dB"
Set playback gain in dB. Default zero.
.TP
.B "\-p milliseconds, \-\-playout milliseconds"
Set initial playout delay in milliseconds. Default 100.
.TP
.B "\-l location, \-\-location location"
Where you are, as lat,lon (42.835,\-78.607) or a locator (FN02qu); also
location in the [display] section of the configuration file. Positions heard
are then shown with their distance and bearing from here, and of the
repeaters.d entries for one frequency and tone the nearest is used.
.TP
.B "\-n, \-\-notch"
Enable CTCSS tone notching. CTCSS/DCS detection and display are independent
of this option.
.TP
.B "\-s, \-\-voting"
Enable automatic session selection; also disables automatic stereo
positioning.
.TP
.B "\-S, \-\-autosort"
Automatically sort the session display.
.TP
.B "\-q, \-\-quiet"
Disable the curses display.
.TP
.B "\-u milliseconds, \-\-update milliseconds"
Set curses display update timeout in integer milliseconds. Default 100.
.TP
.B "\-f pathname, \-\-config pathname"
Read INI configuration first, then apply command-line overrides. Sections
include [audio], [display], [radio], and [repeater].
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-verbose"
Enable diagnostics.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Print version and exit.
.SH INTERACTION
Use up/down to select sessions. m/u mute/unmute the selected session; M/U act
on all sessions. r resets the selected playout buffer and R resets all
buffers. + and - changes volume; left/right arrows changes stereo position.
Uppercase Q exits; lowercase q toggles quiet display. The 'h' key toggles
a help screen.
.SH TALKGROUP NAMES
During a P25 call, monitor shows the talkgroup's name and description if
the talkgroup number is listed in a .csv file in
/var/lib/ka9q-radio/talkgroups.d. The files need "Decimal" and "Alpha Tag"
columns, as in a talkgroup download from radioreference.com; see
pcmrecord(1). A subdirectory there holds the files of one radio system, of
any kind: the one whose channel has "system = " and the subdirectory's name
in radiod's configuration. Calls on that system take their names from that
subdirectory alone. After the call the name stays, with the time since the call
ended, until another call takes its place.
.PP
Between calls a P25 trunking follower (radiod's p25-follow) shows "CC" and
the NAC while it is decoding the control channel, "no CC" when it has
decoded nothing for two seconds, and "CC search" while it tries the site's
other control channel frequencies (the frequency shown changes as it does).
.SH NOTES
A session may not appear until data arrives, particularly when receiver
squelch suppresses output. Missing metadata can prevent interpretation of
dynamically assigned payload formats.
.SH EXAMPLES
List playback devices and listen to an output group:
.PP
.nf
monitor \-L
monitor hf\-pcm.local
monitor \-q \-I hf\-pcm.local \-I vhf\-pcm.local
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
radiod(8), control(1), pcmrecord(1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/ka9q/ka9q-radio
